$topic{'ide'} = {
		title => 'IDE', category => 'apps', app => 'ide',
		templates => [ 'templates/apps/coding_chat.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/coding.js' ],
		summary => 'A colour-coded editor for Perl, JavaScript, HTML and templates.',
		verified => '2026-11-02',
		body => [
			'IDE edits code with syntax colouring for Perl, JavaScript, HTML and templates. It is what the project is developed in from inside itself.',
			'The code button in the toolbar opens the Coding agent\'s own chatbox: ask it what to change and it edits the repo, checks the change and ships it, reporting each step. It is the same agent the Guide\'s Coding toggle drives, and the editor keeps its own conversation. The + in its bar opens another agent, each with its own thread, and the bar drags the panel anywhere on the screen — it floats over the windows like the Guide\'s associate, and it reopens where you left it. A run keeps going in its own process: reloading the page or restarting the tree does not lose it, the panel picks the run back up when it returns, and stop in its bar asks it to finish the step it is on. Beside each step, thinking opens the model\'s working for that turn, the answer is written into the panel as it arrives, and the foot of the log says what the run is doing now. A task can also be handed to a subagent: it investigates or makes an edit inside the paths it is given, reports back, and never ships - the finishing routine stays with the agent you gave the task to. The subagent limit is set beside the Coding model in Configure → Plat → Coding: ten by default, and zero turns subagents off. A fix that has shipped reaches the page on its own: a stylesheet or template change lands in place, while a change to the manager\'s scripts or its shell asks once with a press-to-update note (a hidden tab reloads itself, and a chat in the panel survives it). Double-press the Assistant button for the Agents list: every coding conversation and guide associate, named, with history a press away — and the panels you left open come back with it.',
			'That chatbox also holds the error watch\'s staging area: press fixes in its corner to see every error caught in the running tree\'s log (switch the watch on under Configure → Plat → Self-healing), each with a fix the Coding agent has proposed. Apply writes the proposal, checks it compiles, and gives it the same finishing routine a chat change gets — bump, commit, push — and the panel\'s restart link puts it live. Forget throws a row away, and nothing is written until Apply. Every fix that ships is recorded: Fixed so far in Configure → Plat → Self-healing keeps the list, and review shows it.',
			'Only the top of the tree loads at first; a folder brings its own contents in when you unfold it, so a big project opens quickly. The tree reopens with the folders you had unfolded still open, the window returns to whichever pane — the list or the editor — you left it on, and the file list and each open file keep their place, so you come back to where you were.'
		],
		steps => [
			'Open the IDE and choose a file from the project tree; unfold a folder to see what is in it.',
			'Edit with syntax colouring and the code helpers.',
			'Save; a Perl or JavaScript change needs a restart to take effect, while a template change is live at once.',
			'Press the code button to open the Coding agent and describe a change in words. Its stop control asks it to finish the step it is on without dropping the run.',
			'Drag the panel by its bar to move it; the + in the bar opens another agent, with its own conversation.',
			'Press fixes in its corner to review errors caught in the log and apply a proposed fix.'
		],
		related => [ 'editor', 'terminal' ]
	};

$topic{'weather'} = {
		title => 'Weather', category => 'apps', app => 'weather',
		templates => [ 'templates/apps/weather.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/style.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/now.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/history.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/accuracy.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/automations.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/map.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/weather.js', 'public/js/manager/weather_history.js', 'public/js/manager/weather_automation.js', 'public/js/manager/weather_map.js' ],
		verified => '2026-11-08',
		summary => 'Current conditions and a seven-day forecast for the phone, tablet or machine you choose.',
		body => [
			'Weather reads the house\'s own GPS fixes, not a fixed city page. The picker lists places, not machines: machines reporting the same ground share one entry, and each entry is named after the place the weather service reads for (Kitchener rather than a coordinate), so the list reads as places. The location dropdown and Add stay at the top of the window while a tab scrolls, so the place you are reading stays in view. Hover an entry to see which machines are there. A saved place within thirty kilometres of a fix is where that reading belongs, so a nearby town does not become a second place. The house\'s home plate is its own station: it sits first in the picker, marked always checked, and the hourly pass always reads there, whatever phone is away. Moving the home plate to a new place unanchors the old home; it stays in Places as an added place, and Anchor keeps it recording if you want it. The app\'s display name follows the new home when it still reads as the old home\'s name; a name you set yourself is left alone. Choosing a place asks Environment Canada for the nearest city when it is in Canada, the US National Weather Service when it is in the United States, and Open-Meteo anywhere else. A Canadian fix therefore names the nearest Environment Canada city instead of an hour-away city page. A place outside Canada and the United States is named by a reverse geocoder (BigDataCloud), and the answer is kept, so the picker reads a place rather than a coordinate.',
			'The current card shows the temperature, condition, feels like, high and low, wind and gusts, humidity, pressure, dew point, visibility and UV. Below it are the next 24 hours, which scroll sideways, and a fourteen-day forecast: the national service\'s week, then Open-Meteo\'s later days, with an alert banner when the provider has one. Each day reads as its weekday over a month/day/year stamp, and a day past this week says Next before the weekday. Tap a day to open its detail: the hours when precipitation is likely, a small temperature chart shaded where precipitation is likely, the warmest and coolest hours, and the day\'s sunrise, sunset, UV and wind; tap it again to close. The foot names Open-Meteo when later days came from it. The foot names the provider, the distance to the city when Environment Canada was used, and when the reading was fetched.',
			'Use browser location asks the browser for a one-off coordinate; it is not written to the GPS record. It reads the weather there without keeping a place. When you are away from every saved place, Check weather here keeps that reading and adds the place. Refresh asks the provider again instead of using the ten-minute cache. A device with no fresh fix says so and leaves the other machines in the picker.',
			'Add opens the same canvas map Travel uses, with its tile provider and measured maps. Tap the map to place the flag, tap again to move it, and Save adds that coordinate to Places; tick Keep recording here to anchor it. Cancel closes the map and returns to the view you had. Leave the name empty and the place is named after the weather service\'s nearest city, or by a reverse geocoder outside Canada and the United States, never a coordinate. The hourly pass does not follow the phone: it reads the home plate and the places you anchored, and Check weather here adds a place when you are away from all of them, so a visit is a place you can find later. Anchor, beside Delete, keeps the selected place in the hourly pass; press it again to stop checking it every hour. Delete removes the place you are looking at and tombstones its readings; press it twice. The same Delete sits beside At in a rule, and a rule that named the removed place reads as a deleted place until you choose another. The date beside a place is its latest fix, written month/day/year and hour:minute:second.',
			'The provider follows the coordinate. To force one, set the misc setting weather_provider to ec, nws or openmeteo; leave it empty for automatic.',
			'The History tab reads the old weather appointments and the app\'s own readings back to the start of the house\'s records, folding them into one row per day with the high, low, mean, condition, wind and forecast humidity the old app logged. Each source is named for its place — Warwick Weather, Charlottetown Weather — so the list reads as places rather than app names. History source picks which of them to read; the app chooses the one whose city matches the forecast. The app\'s own source follows the place on screen: pick the phone and its days are the phone\'s, pick a saved place and they are that place\'s. Each place the station has readings for keeps its own source — Warwick Weather, Toronto Weather — so a home that moved keeps its old days under the old place instead of hiding them behind the new home. Readings the old apps kept never named a place, so they stay in every view. A place you delete is tombstoned with its readings, so History does not keep what you removed. Backfill history rebuilds that view. Choose 1 day (hourly) to read the last 24 hours as one temperature per hour instead of one row per day. Actual high and low are the extremes of the readings the house kept, so they are sampled rather than a daily extreme.',
			'The Accuracy tab compares each daily forecast the Weather app recorded, made one to seven days before the date, with what the house actually measured. The summary gives the mean absolute error for the high and low and the condition match for each lead day; the detail table shows the selected lead. Forecast snapshots begin when this app first loads, because the old app kept only seven raw pages, not its forecasts. Choose the history source that matches the forecast city for a fair comparison.',
			'The Automations tab keeps the rules that turn the weather into an app. A condition rule names a word the condition must contain, the app to start, and whether to start it or only record it. When the condition no longer contains the word, the app is stopped. The rain, snow, fog, sun and thunder rules from the old weather app are carried over the first time this tab or the Now tab reads a condition; the old page keeps its hourly readings but stops driving apps, so the rules follow the house rather than a city an hour away. Each rule also names the place it is about: At lists Any location, the home plate, each place the house\'s machines are on (machines at one place share an entry) and the places you saved or anchored. A rule with a place acts only on a reading taken there, so rain on the phone and rain at the cottage are separate rules; the rules carried over from the old app are kept at the home place, and the tab shows the rules for the location chosen in the picker above, so each place has its own list. A rule that says Any location applies everywhere and stays in every view. A device entry is read when you press Check now; the home plate, anchored places and places a rule names are read every hour. The app field suggests the house\'s apps as you type and shows their names. The house server checks on its own about every hour at the home plate and each anchored or rule-named place, once for the whole household, and it is the machine that starts or stops an app, so one reading is one action however many screens show the weather. If the house server goes away, the other machines choose one of themselves to keep the pass and the rules running until it returns, and once the whole house is back together the app folds the duplicate readings that split made. On another machine Check now asks the provider immediately and keeps the reading for the location on screen; the house server applies the rules.',
			'Measurement automations read the values the app records: wind gust, wind speed, wind direction, pressure, temperature, feels like, humidity, visibility, dew point, UV and condition. Add conditions with a comparison (a gust above 20 km/h, a pressure below 101.2 kPa, a wind from the North), and all of them must be true. At works the same way as on a condition rule, so a cabin gust and a phone gust can be different rules. A rule can start an app, stop an app, or run a command. Give it a duration to require the condition to hold for minutes or hours; the window starts counting at the first reading the house keeps, so a six-hour rule begins six hours after that reading, and only readings from the rule\'s place count. A start rule also stops its app when the condition clears; a run rule fires once each time the condition becomes true. Commands run on the house server, which applies the rules.',
		],
		steps => [
			'Open Weather from the start menu.',
			'Choose a place (This device, another place the house\'s machines are on, or a saved place).',
			'Read the current card, the next 24 hours and the fourteen-day forecast; tap a day to open its hour-by-hour detail.',
			'Press Use browser location for a one-off coordinate, or Refresh to ask the provider again. Away from every saved place, Check weather here keeps a reading where you are.',
			'Press Add beside the location dropdown, tap the map to place the flag, leave the name empty to let the weather service name it, tick Keep recording here to anchor it, and Save. Press Anchor to keep a place in the hourly pass, or Delete to remove it and its readings.',
			'Open History to see the last eleven months of readings; choose 1 day (hourly) for the last 24 hours, or press Backfill history if the view is empty.',
			'Open Automations to see the rules for the location you have chosen. Add a condition rule or a measurement rule; it begins at that location, and At changes it. Press Check now to run the rules at once.',
			'Open Accuracy, choose a lead day and compare the forecast high, low and condition with the measured day.',
		],
		related => [ 'configure', 'travel' ]
	};

$topic{'power'} = {
		title => 'Power', category => 'apps', app => 'power',
		templates => [ 'templates/apps/power.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/power.js', 'public/js/manager/taskbar_power.js', 'templates/manager.html.ep', 'public/css/manager.css' ],
		verified => '2026-11-09',
		summary => 'Battery, CPU speed, the charge limit, saved power plans, and sleep and display settings.',
		body => [
			'Power reads this machine\'s battery and CPU and keeps one plan for each power source. The battery card shows the charge, whether it is charging, discharging or full, and the time left (or the time to full while it is charging), taken from the pack\'s own gauge where it reports one. A machine with no battery simply hides that card; a machine with no CPU speed control says so where the CPU figures would be.',
			'The two plan cards are On mains and On battery. Each has a power plan - Auto, Power saver, Balanced or Performance - and a CPU speed limit from 25% to 100%. Save stores that source\'s plan; when the machine is already on that source the plan is applied at once, and the other card waits for the day the cable changes. With Switch plans automatically on, the house applies the saved plan whenever the source changes, so plugging in a laptop or pulling the cable from the Gou moves the CPU without anyone opening the app.',
			'A plan is a governor choice, an energy preference and a turbo choice, tried in an order that fits the kernel: Power saver prefers powersave, asks for the power energy preference and turns turbo off; Balanced prefers schedutil, asks for balance_performance and leaves turbo on; Performance prefers performance. Auto writes nothing and leaves the kernel - or auto-cpufreq, when it is installed - in charge. Choosing any other plan also stops and disables a competing power daemon - power-profiles-daemon, TLP or auto-cpufreq - because two managers re-applying their own profile would race; with Auto they are left alone. The CPU speed limit caps the clock inside whatever governor is chosen, so it works on kernels that offer only one governor. The CPU panel shows the governor, energy preference, driver, number of clock domains, the current and maximum speed in GHz, and the hardware maximum, after each change.',
			'Above the plans, Power says who is managing the power right now: JawnOS and the plan it applied, or a competing daemon - auto-cpufreq, TLP or power-profiles-daemon - that is still running. Auto leaves such a daemon in charge; choosing another plan stops and disables it so the two are not racing.',
			'When the pack reports a charge ceiling - the laptops that stop charging at a chosen percentage - the battery card carries a Charge limit slider from 50% to 100%. Moving it writes the battery\'s own charge_control_end_threshold, so the pack stops there even when the cable stays in; a battery without that attribute hides the row instead of offering a control that writes nowhere.',
			'On the JawnOS live image and the desktop app the power files are the same Linux sysfs the rest of the house uses; the live image\'s passwordless sudo is used when the files are root-owned. On the Gou the same path covers its battery and CPU. On a phone, the battery comes from the app\'s own battery bridge; Android gives an app no way to change the CPU governor, so the CPU panel says the plan is not available there rather than pretending it changed. A machine that can power itself also shows System power here - Restart, Shut down and Sleep - the same fixed actions the Leave menu offers; a phone shows none of them, because it cannot power itself.',
			'When the taskbar is showing, a Battery widget can ride on it: a small pack glyph, the percentage and the time left or to full. It hides itself on a machine with no battery. A press opens a small panel with the battery summary and the power profile for the source the machine is on - Auto, Power saver, Balanced or Performance - so the profile can be changed without opening the app; Open Power at the foot opens this window. Add items -> Widgets puts it on the bar or takes it off, its right-click menu chooses Icon, Icon and status, or Disabled, and edit mode moves it like the clock or the window list.',
			'Sleep and display is the card below the plans. Its Power profile selector names the profile in effect, and the four controls below it belong to that profile: each of Auto, Power saver, Balanced and Performance keeps its own timers and lid action, so switching the plan switches them too. Screen off after chooses 1, 2, 5, 10, 15 or 30 idle minutes, or Never; it is set through the desktop\'s gsettings where that exists, or xset on an X display, and a machine with neither says the screen timer is not available. Sleep after chooses 1, 2, 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60 idle minutes, or Never, and is written to a logind drop-in together with the lid action. When the lid closes appears only on a machine with a real lid: Suspend, Hibernate, Hybrid suspend, Shut down or Do nothing. Sleep type offers only the states this machine reports - Suspend, Hibernate, Hybrid suspend and Suspend then hibernate - and Sleep now enters the chosen state at once. A profile that is not the one the machine is on is only stored; its timers take effect when that plan does.',
		],
		steps => [
			'Open Power from the start menu, or press the battery widget in the taskbar and then Open Power in its panel.',
			'Read the battery card: charge, state and the time left or to full.',
			'If the battery offers a Charge limit, drag the slider to the percentage it should stop at; the pack keeps that ceiling when plugged in.',
			'On On mains, choose the plan and the CPU speed limit the machine should use while plugged in, then press Save.',
			'On On battery, choose the plan and limit for battery use, then press Save.',
			'Leave Switch plans automatically on to apply the matching plan when the cable changes; turn it off to switch only when you press Apply now.',
			'Press Apply now to use the current source\'s saved plan immediately.',
			'On a machine that can power itself, press Restart, Shut down or Sleep in System power; a phone has no such row.',
			'Read the CPU panel to see the governor, energy preference, driver and clock speed after a change.',
			'In Sleep and display, choose the Power profile to edit, then when the screen turns off and when the system sleeps, then press Save; each profile keeps its own timers and lid action.',
			'If the machine has a lid, choose what closing it does; a machine without a lid has no such row.',
			'Choose a Sleep type this machine offers and press Sleep now to enter that state immediately.',
		],
		prompt => 'The reader is using JawnOS\'s Power app. It has one battery card, two plan cards (On mains and On battery), an automatic-switch tick, a Sleep and display card and a CPU panel. A plan is Auto, Power saver, Balanced or Performance, and each source also has a CPU speed limit. Saving the source the machine is on applies it at once; the other source is stored until the cable changes. Sleep and display holds a Power profile selector plus the screen timeout, the sleep timeout, the lid-close action and the sleep type for that profile; each profile keeps its own block; the lid row exists only on a machine with a lid, and the sleep type offers only states this machine reports. Never claim a CPU speed, a screen timer or a sleep state changed on a machine where the panel says it is unavailable, and never describe a phone as changing a performance mode its app does not offer. If unsure, describe the control\'s label and what the panel reports rather than inventing a sequence.',
		related => [ 'taskbar', 'start_menu', 'configure' ]
	};

